{"step_index":115,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T10:31:00Z","thinking":"Decommissioning lacks a standard procedure; this is a fresh, official effort to define the complete lifecycle. The workflow diagram needs a detailed examination to understand the process. Each block must be individually analyzed for its actor and designated role within the decommissioning process.\n\n\nThe initial phase centers on the formal request to decommission a resource, originating from a stakeholder and documented via ticketing. Next, a detailed impact analysis is crucial, involving dependency mapping across network flows, DNS, proxies, databases, cron jobs, and associated certificates to assess the risks and define a rollback plan. Finally, the proposed decommissioning undergoes review and approval by a change advisory board, which verifies impacts and approves the go-ahead for the process.\n\n\nOfficial commencement of the operational phase requires notifying stakeholders following CAB approval. The inventory system needs updating to reflect the change in equipment/VM status to either decommissioning or offline, and also record the planned removal date. Subsequently, the monitoring and alerting systems should be configured for downtime or disabled completely to prevent false alarms during the decommissioning. Finally, a complete, verified, and securely archived final backup will be performed.\n\n\nEnsuring the backup's integrity is the crucial prerequisite before initiating the irreversible decommissioning steps. The first technical action involves an orderly shutdown of the application services and operating system. A quarantine period will follow to confirm the absence of any unforeseen impacts.\n\nTemporary firewall rule disabling is necessary to facilitate a quick rollback if undocumented network flows are encountered. This step involves disabling or commenting out the firewall rules linked to the machine's IP address. Finally, the virtual machine will be permanently deleted from the hypervisor, or physically decommissioned if bare metal, involving secure disk erasure. Storage will then be deallocated.\n\n\nFollowing application shutdown, resource reclamation is next, including CPU/RAM release, IP address handling, and DNS record removal. Subsequently, firewall rules are permanently deleted by the security team, cleaning up network filtering and security zones. Finally, the decommissioning status is updated, a report is drafted and submitted, and relevant tickets are closed, with final communication to involved parties. The document metadata confirms Amit SAMY, or Infra Linux, authored the procedure for server and infrastructure equipment decommissioning.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport requests\\n\\nTOKEN = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpZCI6IjY2ZDAwY2I0LWEzY2YtNDIyYS1hMjQ5LWVkMzA2NzEwZDdjZiIsImV4cGlyZXNBdCI6IjIwMjYtMTItMDhUMTA6NDU6MjcuNzkzWiIsInR5cGUiOiJzZXNzaW9uIiwic2VydmljZSI6ImF6dXJlIiwiaWF0IjoxNzg4ODY0MzI3fQ.tQTTmIeOP_H2i-10PROwjV-c_snixZFU5pG6CpKXXCk'\\nBASE_URL = 'https://wiki.corp.intranet.local'\\n\\nres = requests.post(f'{BASE_URL}/api/attachments.redirect', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {TOKEN}'}, json={'id': 'cd659696-41ea-4769-94d8-369aa52f8717'}, verify=False, allow_redirects=False)\\nprint('Status:', res.status_code, 'Headers:', res.headers.get('Location'))\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/home/koby\"","RunPersistent":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Testing attachments.redirect directly\"","toolSummary":"\"Test attachments.redirect\""}}]}
